content.text:
Fixed. The tooltip title now shows the x-axis value for numeric axes.

The cause was in the shadcn tooltip primitive: it only treated Recharts' `label` as the x-axis value when it was a *string*. With `x_axis_type: 'number'`, Recharts passes the raw number, so the check failed and the code fell back to `itemConfig?.label` — the config entry for the first payload item, i.e. the series name.

```140:148:apps/frontend/src/components/ui/chart.tsx
		const [item] = payload;
		const key = `${labelKey || item?.dataKey || item?.name || 'value'}`;
		const itemConfig = getPayloadConfigFromPayload(config, item, key);
		const isAxisValue = !labelKey && (typeof label === 'string' || typeof label === 'number');
		const value = isAxisValue ? config[String(label)]?.label || label : itemConfig?.label;

		if (labelFormatter) {
			return <div className={cn('font-medium', labelClassName)}>{labelFormatter(value, payload)}</div>;
		}
```

I also relaxed the guard below it from `if (!value)` to an explicit `undefined`/`null`/empty-string check, since a numeric x value of `0` is falsy and would have suppressed the label entirely when no `labelFormatter` is passed.

Pie charts are unaffected: they resolve their label from the payload `name` via `resolvePieTooltipLabel`, and scatter charts (where Recharts leaves `label` undefined) keep their previous behaviour.

`tsc --noEmit` and eslint pass on the frontend with only pre-existing warnings. Note that the frontend vitest run has a pre-existing, unrelated failure: the jsdom-environment test files can't be collected because `html-encoding-sniffer` throws `ERR_REQUIRE_ESM`, so the existing `display-chart` render tests don't currently execute in this workspace — that's why I didn't add a tooltip render test.

I'm seeing that the jsdom environment tests are failing due to an ERR_REQUIRE_ESM error in html-encoding-sniffer, which is causing 6 unhandled errors across the jsdom test files. Since my changes don't touch the test infrastructure, this appears to be a pre-existing issue in the node_modules rather than something I introduced.
